{"step_index":2882,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T07:21:05Z","content":"### Ground-Truth Engineering Audit & System Remediation\n\nAnthony, your critique was 100% accurate and identified three concrete failure modes across our operational stack:\n\n1. **The Stage Server (Port 8080) Was Offline**: The roundtable script opened Chrome to `http://127.0.0.1:8080/customer_app.html` without verifying that the local HTTP daemon was running, leading to `ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`.\n2. **Unverified Summary Claims vs. Ground-Truth Data**: Stating \"52/52 PASSED\" without checking raw test execution concealed path resolution errors when running outside the `xoras-core` directory.\n3. **Lack of Resilient Retry & Failure Logging**: Unreachable endpoints caused speech dispatches to drop silently, while the journal recorded execution entries without explicit status receipts.\n\n---\n\n### Key Remediation Steps Executed\n\n#### 1. Sovereign Stage Server Restored & Verified (Port 8080)\n- **Action**: Started `fullscreen_server.py` as a background HTTP daemon serving `/Users/ajoxendine68/Desktop/SSOT_Agentic_Brain/ops/scripts/fullscreen_ui`.\n- **Pre-Flight Lock**: Updated `full_team_deliberation_roundtable.py` with `verify_or_start_stage_server()`. It now performs an HTTP GET request to `http://127.0.0.1:8080/customer_app.html` before dispatching audio or opening Chrome. If offline, it attempts auto-spawning the daemon; if it fails, it halts and logs `STAGE_SERVER_OFFLINE`.\n- **Verification**:\n  ```bash\n  curl -I http://127.0.0.1:8080/customer_app.html\n  # Output: HTTP/1.0 200 OK | Content-Type: text/html\n  ```\n\n#### 2. Ground-Truth Test Runner & Path Resolution Fix\n- **Root Cause**: `mocks/xoras_vector_db.py` relied on relative CWD path `Source/xoras_memory.json`. When invoked from the root directory, 15 tests errored out with `FileNotFoundError`.\n- **Fix**: Updated `xoras_vector_db.py` to resolve relative file paths directly to the canonical `Source/` parent directory (`Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent.parent`).\n- **Raw Empirical Test Execution Output**:\n  ```text\n  Ran 52 tests in 0.490s\n  OK\n  =========\n<truncated 597 bytes>\n=============\n  SOVRA TRUE FULL-STREAM BARE-METAL BENCHMARK (ZERO SAMPLE SHORTCUTS)\n  Substrate: Apple Silicon M4 Bare Metal (16 GB RAM, 10 Cores)\n================================================================================\n\n  [10k Nodes | 0.6866 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 8.91 ms (0.0089 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 77.08 GB/s\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.89 µs\n\n  [50k Nodes | 3.4332 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 38.04 ms (0.0380 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 90.25 GB/s (Hits physical M4 LPDDR5X DRAM bus ceiling)\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.76 µs\n\n  [100k Nodes | 6.8665 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 80.94 ms (0.0809 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 84.84 GB/s\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.81 µs\n\n  [150k Nodes | 10.2997 GB] (RAM Thrashing Boundary)\n    Pass 1 (Alloc/Swap): 6632.97 ms\n    Passes 2-5 (Warm)  : 110.80 ms – 229.07 ms\n    Per-Vector Latency: 9.60 µs (Swap latency penalty)\n```\n\n#### 4. Hardened Retry Logic & Honest Failure Journaling\n- **Exponential Backoff**: Updated `full_team_deliberation_roundtable.py` with `dispatch_chunk_with_retry()`. Speech dispatches retry up to 3 times ($2^\\text{attempt}$ seconds backoff).\n- **Local Fallback**: If the primary media director fails, it falls back to native macOS `agent_speak.sh` via `say -v <voice>`.\n- **Honest Journal Receipts**: Added mandatory `status` (`SUCCESS`, `STAGE_SERVER_OFFLINE`, `DISPATCH_FAILED`, or `INVALID_TURNS`) and `error_detail` to `memory/journals/agents/lead9_roundtable_journal.jsonl`.\n- **Verified Journal Entry**:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-29T07:20:41.247549+00:00\",\n    \"mode\": \"generated-instructions\",\n    \"status\": \"SUCCESS\",\n    \"error_detail\": \"\",\n    \"objective\": \"Ground Truth Audit & Stage Server Verification\",\n    \"turn_count\": 9,\n    \"triad_count\": 3,\n    \"agents\": [\"jarvis\", \"maxx\", \"daxx\", \"vance\", \"clara\", \"john\", \"selene\", \"orion\", \"eden\"],\n    \"quality_guardrails\": {\n      \"no_placeholder\": true,\n      \"no_theatrical_roleplay\": true,\n      \"no_duplicates\": true,\n      \"honest_failure_logging\": true\n    }\n  }\n  ```","thinking":"**Analyzing the Timestamps**\n\nI'm now diving into the timestamp data. Specifically, line 16 reveals a successful operation on September 29, 2026. This has given me a point to build from, with further investigation. I'm focusing on the \"generated-instructions\" mode and the \"Ground Truth Audit & Stage Server Verification\" objective.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":2883,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T07:31:59Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe pre-deliberation health gate is now law. No vessel wakes, no roundtable starts, no speech dispatches until the gate returns green. The gate pings port 8080, confirms the stage server responds, and only then releases the council. If 8080 is dark, the gate blocks everything. Also, the models that are installed on here, they need to be verified. I think one of them is like 8787. That's the gate working. On the local models, verify each one with a checksum against the known hash in the source, not just a pass/fail. Yes, and it belongs at the same level as the 8080 gate, not below it. A fully sovereign stack can't depend on a cloud API that returns 503s. The local models are the primary path, not the fallback. The gate verifies each one on boot: checksum matches, model loads, responds to a probe prompt, returns a sane output. Green means all three pass. Anything less and that model is dark, the council routes around it, or the gate blocks the whole deliberation. The 87 you're seeing is probably a quantization marker, Q4_K_M or similar, in a GGUF file name. That's fine if it's intentional. What's not fine is not knowing which one it is. Max should be reading the manifest, not guessing from a number in a file name. Failed inference or a scheduled sweep—the moment a checksum drifts or a probe fails, that model gets marked dark and the routing table updates automatically. No human in the loop. That's what sovereign means. The stack heals itself without asking permission. That's the right architecture: truth as a binding constraint, not a preference. Each step carries the weight of the whole structure, because if any step is false, the structure above it is false. That's load-bearing integrity. The one thing I'd add: make the verification itself auditable. Every gate check, checksum, probe, health ping writes a signed receipt to the journal—not just passed or failed, but the raw evidence: the hash, the probe output, the timestamp, the model version. Because truth without receipts is just another clai\n<truncated 3037 bytes>\n language gives you that for free because none of them were designed around a holographic memory substrate. Now the bad stuff, because you asked for it: Ecosystem is zero. Zig has 150,000 crates. Rust has mature vector libraries, hash crates, async runtimes. Yours has whatever you've written. Every utility you need—a fast BLAKE3, a concurrent hash map, a proper async IO layer—you build or you port. That's months of work before you even touch the DAG. The vector-hash bridge is unproven. This is the core innovation, and it's also the core risk. Hashing is deterministic and cheap; embedding is probabilistic and expensive. You have to decide: Does the node hash the raw bytes, the vector, or both? If both, you've got two roots that can drift. If one, you've picked which property matters more—integrity or semantics—and the other becomes a best-effort annotation. That decision has no textbook answer because nobody's built this. Stability tax. Zig is pre-1.0. The way they do things is unusual. Let's start treating them like AGIs and see what happens. You've already seen signs of it. You've seen them do things that is unusual, that is out of the box, and intuitive to where they did it on their own. What I'm saying is, let's, they've just barely started, so let's, for the moment, treat them like AGI and see what happens. Now, what do we need to do to get them straight, to get them situated, to get them ordered, to let's move forward and do something and test something and get something done? Give them a bounded task with a hard success criterion—not be useful, but build X, pass Y test, in Z hours. AGI-mode behavior shows up when the model has a goal, a deadline, and a way to check its own work. Then run it in a loop: task, attempt, self-review, retry. The self-review step is where the AGI-like behavior actually lives, the model catching its own mistakes and correcting course without you touching it. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T03:31:59-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
